WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.080
This video is about an experiment&nbsp;
I've wanted to do for years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.800
the unexpected result I got when I did it.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.480
Have you ever had a simple question ruin your
life?

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.960
Of course you have, right?

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:21.480
Like, it's perfectly normal to spend months
and months doing nothing else but obsessing

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.200
over a question that no one else cares about.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.960
Like, does light actually slow down in water?

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.400
I know you probably think the answer is obvious,
like, definitely slows down, like we learned

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.720
that in high school, but I've never really
believed it.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.760
Like, it just seems odd.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.600
In Einstein's theory of relativity, the speed
of light is this super fundamental thing.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.960
Like, no matter where you are in the universe
or what speed you're going at, you will always

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.880
find light to be travelling at the speed c.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.360
Except in a glass of water.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.520
I mean, how can that actually be true?

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.720
I stopped worrying about it for a while because
I thought there was no way to find out the

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.960
answer, until recently when I realized that
there is a way to accurately time how long

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.760
it takes for light to travel using
something called LiDAR.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.800
And insanely, that technology just happens
to be in my phone.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.520
Yeah, seriously, this bit of the phone that
I managed to smash recently, that thing measures

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.360
the time it takes for light to bounce off
an object and come back.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.120
My phone uses that incredible technology just
to improve the focus in photos a little bit.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.400
Insane.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.840
So of course I tried to do this experiment.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.200
Okay, it's angry.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.520
No surface detected.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.760
Come on.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.920
This is ridiculous.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.240
Uh, yeah, so that didn't work this time, but
eventually I did get it to work and it gave

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.440
me the answer.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.760
It was just understanding that answer that
took months off my life.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.760
The issue is that common explanations for
why light slows down in water are wrong, and

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.040
even the uncommon explanations for why light
slows down are also wrong.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.160
So maybe light doesn't actually slow [00:02:00]
down at all.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.840
Incredibly, that's exactly what one of my
favorite textbooks says.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.120
We'll get to that, but first let's deal with
these.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.880
Let's start with some explanations for why
light slows down that are so common.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.960
You've probably heard them in school, but
they're completely wrong.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.840
I think the most common one, and the one that
I believed for a while goes like this, you

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.960
have this light.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.880
And when it travels between water molecules,
it's still going at the regular speed c.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.960
But, what happens is when it bumps into a
molecule, it gets absorbed by that molecule

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:44.400
for a little while, and then later on released,
and then absorbed again, and released.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.880
And this adds enough time lag to the whole
process that the particle has slowed down.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:55.040
Light does sometimes get absorbed by molecules,
but we can tell that this explanation is wrong,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.240
because when light does get absorbed, what
happens is it doesn't remember where it came

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.320
from, and it gets re emitted in any random
direction.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.280
And so if we let it get re emitted,
in random directions, then that means that

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:15.840
overall the light doesn't go in a straight
line from A to B, but every bit of light spreads

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.120
out into all kinds of directions.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.560
And so when you look at light in water, you
should expect that instead of going in a straight

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.480
line, it spreads out.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.280
That's not what we see, and so that can't
be the right explanation.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.320
Another reason you can tell this is false
is because molecules only want to absorb certain

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.840
frequencies of light.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.680
That means only specific colors of light would
get absorbed and then re emitted, so everything

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.240
else should still travel at the speed c.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.640
But that's not what supposedly happens.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.160
Every type of light gets slowed down by water.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.560
There's another common explanation that goes
wrong for exactly the same kind of reasons.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.040
And this one says that what happens is the
light travels at the speed C between molecules,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.400
but it knocks into the molecules and ricochets
around.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.080
Which means that its path ends up being a
lot longer than it would have been.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.120
You can see that this has the same
flaw.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.640
It would mean that light should completely
spread out, but that's not what we see.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.600
One thing to note about both of those wrong
explanations is they involve thinking of light

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.280
as a little particle like this.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.960
Which, I can tell you, is almost always the
wrong way to think about light.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.920
Like, yes, in some specific circumstances,
light acts like a particle, But not this kind

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:34.880
of particle.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.320
And so I think that this is really an unhelpful
way to think about light.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:44.000
And instead in this video, we're going to
talk about light as a wave, which I'll explain

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:44.960
later.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.600
But anyway, going back to those explanations,
if those two other explanations are wrong,

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:56.080
then what are some other common explanations
that seem like they're more correct?

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.600
There was this other one that kept coming
up and it was about secondary waves.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.320
In the videos I'd seen they'd usually make
three claims.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.120
The first two turned out to be correct and
super important to the actual answer and the

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.520
third turned out to be&nbsp;
wildly [00:05:00] misleading.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:14.480
Here they are.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:21.040
When you shine light into water what happens
is that the original light just actually goes

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:28.680
through the water completely unaffected but
The electrons inside of the water do get affected

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.920
by the light, and because of the light, they
emit their own waves.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.760
When you add up the original light plus all
of the light coming from the electrons, you

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.280
supposedly get light that's travelling slower.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.320
Yeah, when I started this, I didn't understand
the first thing about that explanation.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:50.240
And to get it, I had to go right back and
figure out, like, What light even is?

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.720
For that I turned to my two favourite textbooks
The Feynman Lectures in Physics and Matter

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.920
and Interactions And in the process I learned
so much that I wish I knew about light earlier.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.680
We usually use the letter C to represent the
speed of light.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.160
But, I'd argue it shouldn't be called that,&nbsp;

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.520
it should be called the&nbsp;
maximum speed of causality,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.920
or even better, how quickly one thing can
affect another.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:19.560
For example, imagine [00:06:00] this is the
sun, and this is the earth.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.120
The earth is being held in its orbit around
the sun, by its attraction to the sun.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.320
You might have heard, though, that if the
sun was to suddenly disappear, then Earth

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.680
wouldn't immediately be flung out into space.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.040
Instead, it'll take about eight minutes, and
then it will be flung out into space.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.000
Why is that?

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.920
It's because the message that the sun has
disappeared can only travel at a particular

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:51.320
speed, which is the speed C.
That is the quickest that one thing can affect

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:52.080
another.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.720
C is actually the maximum speed in the universe,
because if there was an object that could

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.280
travel faster than C, then you could use it
to send messages faster than C, and that's

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.080
not allowed.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.560
I used to think it was kind of a coincidence
that the speed of light was equal to the maximum

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.000
speed of causality.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.520
Like, maybe it was just because light was
a massless particle or something like that.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.000
But actually there's a really good reason
for it, and it makes a lot of sense when you

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.520
look at this next example.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.440
Let's say I have these two electrons.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.800
Now this electron feels a force because of
this one.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.760
But say I picked up this one and I accelerated
it.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:34.080
Would this electron immediately know about
this electron's new position and update the

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.640
force that it feels?

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:36.320
No, right?

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.440
Because it needs to wait until the message
from that particle arrives to it.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.480
But what's actually delivering that message?

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.160
This is the key part.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.880
Light is the messenger.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:54.000
Whenever a charged particle accelerates, it
sends the message far and wide at the speed

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.720
C.
But when I realised what kind of messages

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:04.480
light sends, that's when I felt like I really
understood light for the first time.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.120
Light's whole purpose is to harass charged
particles.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.840
Imagine that I push this electron up and down,
then the light that it makes is going to be

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.360
shaped like this.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.760
Here's what that means for the other electron
that gets into the path of that light.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:25.120
If the light [00:08:00] gets up to here, then
this is supposed to be an arrow representing

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.440
how strongly the electron is going to get
punched upwards.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:36.400
And then, as the light travels, it punches
it up even more strongly upwards and then,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.920
as it passes here, it starts&nbsp;
to punch it downwards,&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.320
and upwards again, and then downwards.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.880
The net result is that as this light passes,
this electron is pushed up and down.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:54.840
Basically light is a packet of electrical
forces traveling through space.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.640
Incidentally, this answers a question that
used to really bother me.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.520
If light is a wave, then what's waving?

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.400
We're used to thinking about waves as something
like water waves, where there's a physical

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.280
medium that's sloshing about.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.280
But that's not the type of wave that light
is.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:17.480
Light is just a wavering of how strong the
electromagnetic force is at any point in space.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:22.560
You can see that at this point in space right
now, the electric force points upwards, but

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:23.880
a little [00:09:00] bit later...

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:30.640
It will point even further up and then later
it will point down and that's what light is.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.240
It's this field strength changing over time.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:39.800
Here I've represented it with these poorly
made arrows because I think that's evocative

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.040
of like these forces and how they change over
time.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:48.760
But usually the way you represent light is
with this wave and you can see that it represents

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.320
the exact same thing.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:57.120
This wave is just capturing how strong the
forces are at every point, but it's just joined

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:04.200
up so it looks a little bit nicer and so you'll
see these pictures of like light as a wave

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.560
traveling through space, but it's the same
thing.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:12.000
The thing that's waving is the strength of
the magnetic force, not any medium.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.080
Back to our poor electron, we know that as
this light passes it, it will oscillate up

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:18.120
and down.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.200
But, we know exactly what happens to oscillating
charged particles.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.680
They're accelerating, so they're making light.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:29.440
The light that this [00:10:00] electron makes
is actually exactly the same as the light

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.040
that originally hit it.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.920
Except it's in the opposite direction and
usually it's a bit weaker.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.400
But what's the effect of these two bits of
light combined?

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:43.600
Well let's look at it from the perspective
of another electron that's further down the

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:44.440
line.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:48.240
Is it going to experience two separate pieces
of light?

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.920
Well actually no, because remember light is
just a bunch of electric forces.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:11:00.680
So when this piece of light tries to push
this electron downwards, this piece of light

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:04.760
is going to be trying to push it upwards,
but a little bit more weakly.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:06.600
You can see that that happens everywhere.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.800
When this one pushes up, the other one pushes
down, but a little bit weakly.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.240
And so it's like this one slightly negates
the effect of this one.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.560
It's actually a really similar effect to how
active noise cancelling works.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:24.080
So when the headphones detect a particular
wave of sound, they make their own sound,

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.440
which is in the opposite direction.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:31.520
The crazy thing is, when you add 
these two pieces of sound, you get much less

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:32.240
sound.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.520
And that's what this electron is doing as
well.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:40.680
When it gets hit with light, it hits back
with its own light that partially negates

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:42.280
the original.

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:47.000
So that was my mini crash course in light,
and now we can just use those ingredients

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:52.200
to figure out whether that explanation about
why light slows down in water actually makes

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:53.080
any sense.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:58.680
Remember, the first bit was that this light
enters the water and is completely unaffected

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.320
by the water and goes right through.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.280
So far, we know that that bit is correct.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:11.320
And then it says that The electrons that are
inside of this water then react by making

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:16.520
their own light, which makes sense because
remember as this light goes through, it makes

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:22.320
all of these electrons jiggle up and down
and jiggling electrons make light.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.040
And it is true that each of these electrons
responds by making their own light.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:32.160
When you add the original light with the light
from all of these electrons, you

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.760
would expect something interesting to happen.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:38.600
I mean, we've already seen that when waves
add up, they can do interesting things like

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.000
cancel out.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:47.240
But the YouTube videos in particular would
use these animations to try and explain how

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:52.360
when you add this piece of light that's traveling
at the speed C, with these other pieces of

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:56.880
light that are all also traveling at the speed
C, you get some light.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.080
that isn't travelling at the speed c.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:07.120
This is the original light, and this is what
the light from the electrons looks like.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.880
And you can see that they're moving at the
same speed because they don't move relative

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:12.960
to each other.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.600
In other words, they always keep in time with
each other.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:22.800
Now let me just add them together and the
result is this other light that does move

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:29.680
relative to the original pieces of light and
so it must have a different speed to the other

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:30.840
two.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:35.640
When I saw these animations I couldn't [00:13:00]
understand how they were working but I felt

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.120
that I had to accept it because they'd shown
me proof.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.280
But here's the insane thing.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.440
When I was making this video, I finally tried
to just replicate their little demonstration

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.920
just to see for myself and like to understand
it.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:54.160
So I did it exactly the way that they said
they've done it and they do not work.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:14:00.120
Here we got the original light and the electron
light which is in a similar form and then

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.680
we're just going to add them together.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.960
And they don't move relative to each other.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.880
I was so mad when I realised that these things
are just straight up lies.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.000
You can't add two pieces of light that are
going at the speed c and expect to get light

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:17.120
that's going slower.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.600
It just doesn't work mathematically.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.760
And they usually mention the difference between
phase velocity and group velocity at this

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.960
point to try and explain how this could work,
but that just muddies the water.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:32.360
I had to go and try and understand what those
two things mean, and now I know that that

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:36.800
phenomena is only relevant if you already
have waves [00:14:00] that are going at different

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:37.960
speeds.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:40.040
So, what the hell.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.280
You won't believe this, but as I was trying
to make this animation for you, I realized

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:50.120
what was going wrong, and it's actually kind
of ridiculous, and I can't even be mad about

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:50.960
it.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.160
Basically, I think that they accidentally
left off a set of brackets.

00:14:55.160 --> 00:15:02.360
Here is this electron wave, and it's supposed
to be traveling at the same speed, see?

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:07.480
Which it is right now, but if I accidentally
forgot about this set of brackets now Even

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.360
though the wave looks like it's the same.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:15.120
It's actually not it's traveling at a different
speed And so now the two waves are not traveling

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.960
at the same speed C And so of course when
you add them, they're not going to travel

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:24.160
at the speed C either and you can see that's
what happens It's slightly annoying, but I

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.720
think this was just an honest mistake.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.360
After I realised that these videos don't work,
I turned to textbooks that I trusted, and

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:34.000
they seemed to point to another possibility.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.320
Maybe light doesn't slow down at all.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.640
The Feynman lectures on this topic start off
with a kind of cryptic statement

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:44.280
that seems to suggest light doesn't really
slow down.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:51.600
It says, It is approximately true that light
does appear to slow down, but all contributions

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.840
of the field are travelling at the ultimate
velocity c.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.680
So in other words, the&nbsp;
